4. What an ORM is in your own words
An ORM (Object Relational Mapper) is software that lets you use Python objects and code instead of writing raw SQL. SQLAlchemy translates my Python code into SQL commands automatically, so I can interact with the database using classes and objects.

5. What db.session.commit() does and why you think it’s needed
db.session.commit() saves the changes permanently into the database file. When you do db.session.add(), the ticket is only staged in memory. Just like in Git where git add stages changes and git commit saves them, db.session.commit() writes the staged changes to the SQLite file so they don’t disappear.

6. What is a primary key and why does every table need one?
A primary key is a unique identifier for each row in a database table. No two rows can have the same primary key. Every table needs one so the database can uniquely identify, update, or delete the correct row, prevent duplicates, and link tables together efficiently.

7. What does nullable=False mean and why would you use it?
nullable=False prevents the value from being empty or NULL. You use it when you want to force the user (or the system) to always provide a value for that field.

