## 2025-06-1
1. API key vs password
A password is for you to log into a website an api key is for your app to log into a service
Same idea, different audience — humans vs. software.

2. What an environment variable is
It’s a secret your computer holds for you outside your code.
Your app can read it, but it never gets saved in your files.

3. Why this is safer
If you hard code a key in your Python file, you might accidentally push it to GitHub.
Env vars keep your secrets off the repo and on your machine only.

4. What export does
export tells the terminal, to make this variable available to any program I run
Without it, Python can’t see your API key.

5. Why the fake key didn’t break anything at first
My Flask route wasn’t actually calling the API yet.
The key only matters when a real request is sent.

6. How I know I am calling the API
This line sends a real request:

python
client.messages.create(...)
That’s when the model runs, the key is checked, and I get billed.

7. What max_tokens does
It controls how long the model’s answer can be.
Small number = short reply.
Big number = longer reply.

8. Why I used Haiku
Haiku is cheap, fast, and perfect for simple tasks.
Using a huge model would cost more for no real benefit here.

Bonus: How teammates get the key
They make their own API key and set it in their environment.
My code never stores the key — it just reads the variable name.

